# Play the game. Skate, jump, spray -- the arm this repo has never had.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# Every other schedule in this directory parks the player. The difference is
# not cosmetic; it is the reason two real defects were invisible for the life
# of the project. Measured 14 Sep, gameplay, same build:
#
#     a person with a controller    on=109  off=103  idle_trap=22371  48% APU
#     every scripted run ever       on=5    off=0    idle_trap=0       0% APU
#
# No scripted run has ever RETIRED a voice. The APU trap storm (48% of audio
# frames lost) and the controller-hotplug abort were both found by hand,
# because a parked player starts no sound effect and finishes none.
#
# TWO GATES, AND THEY ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS
#
#   1. Did the run REACH gameplay?  NtOpenFile count, which needs no probe:
#      ~1342 opens / 260 distinct files / live=133 is the TITLE plateau, and
#      1408 opens / 264 files / live=56 is New Game. A run that stops at 1342
#      never left the attract screen.
#   2. Did it then PLAY?  [APU-VOICE] off=, which must be well clear of zero.
#
# Both gates matter and neither substitutes for the other. The first version of
# this file failed gate 1 and still produced off=1, and off=1 read as "it
# played" until the open count was checked. play_scripted.sh now tests both.
#
# THE BOOT PREFIX IS new_game.pad's, NOT moving.pad's, AND THAT IS THE POINT
#
# moving.pad and still.pad stop pressing START at t=45.5, which depends on
# hitting a single window. Logo length varies by ten seconds or more between
# runs -- that variance is exactly why new_game.pad repeats the group to t=71 --
# so a truncated prefix is a gamble taken fresh on every run. MEASURED: this
# file with moving.pad's prefix reached 1342 opens, i.e. the title plateau, and
# never started a game at all.
#
# RELIABILITY, MEASURED, AND IT IS NOT GOOD: 1 run in 3 reached New Game.
# The two failures sat at exactly 1342 opens, which is the documented "blind
# masher" plateau -- new_game.pad's own header says a one-per-second beat walks
# the title to live=133 / 1342 and stops there. Check the scene gate on EVERY
# run before using it; a title-plateau run still produces a full log and
# plausible audio counters and means nothing.
#
# DO NOT TRY TO FIX THIS BY PRESSING START MORE OFTEN. Tried, 14 Sep: groups
# every 4 s to t=118 instead of every 8 s to t=71, i.e. 27 attempts instead of
# 8. It did not reach New Game either, and denser input is how you become the
# masher that plateaus. There is an explicit note in this project's memory not
# to retune new_game.pad's timings, and it is right. The prefix below is that
# artifact, unchanged.
#
# THE AUDIO DEVICE IS NOT THE VARIABLE. RETRACTED, in three stages, and the
# stages are the lesson:
#
#   1 run   "the one success had a dead audio device" -- a hunch
#   2 v 2   forced off reached New Game twice, live device failed twice.
#           Written up as a clean separation. It was not.
#   n = 6   forced off 2 of 4, live device 0 of 2.
#
# 0/2 against 2/4 is exactly what one ~40% rate looks like. There is no
# evidence the audio device matters. What IS true is that the boot reaches New
# Game roughly two times in five, whatever the audio is doing.
#
# For anything NOT about audio, run with the device forced off and the boot
# becomes reliable. For audio measurements you need it on, and then this
# schedule is back to roughly one run in three -- which is why the trap-storm
# A/B still cannot be taken unattended. See play_scripted.sh's header.
#
# The repeats cost something real and it is written down rather than glossed:
# past the title START is PAUSE, so on a run that was already through, the
# groups at t=52/60/68 pause and unpause, and the A between them lands in a
# pause menu. new_game.pad carries that same risk and is nonetheless the only
# prefix in this tree with a measured record of reaching the tutorial, so this
# file follows the measured artifact rather than a nicer-looking guess. Play
# therefore does not begin until t=80, after the last of them.
#
# PAD-LINT: allow-late-start
# test_pad_schedule.py fails any schedule pressing START past t=48. This file
# is a deliberate exception for the reason above; the play window is placed
# clear of the STARTs so nothing synthetic pauses the part being measured.
#
# WHAT THE BUTTONS ARE
#
# A = jump and RT = spray are the game's own words, not inference: the tutorial
# says "Press the 'A button' and jump 1 time!" and "Just get close to her and
# pull the Right Trigger". The left stick skates. Grinding needs no button --
# you skate onto a rail -- so it is the one thing here a schedule cannot
# guarantee: it depends on where the player happens to be.
#
# HOW THE PARSER ACTUALLY BEHAVES -- READ THIS BEFORE EDITING
#
# Overlapping STICK events on the SAME AXIS do not hand over. The apply path
# (src/input/xinput_device.c:743) writes a scripted deflection only when that
# axis is still near neutral:
#
#     if (ev->lx && st->Gamepad.sThumbLX > -4096 && st->Gamepad.sThumbLX < 4096)
#
# so once an earlier active event has driven the axis to 30000, every later
# overlapping event is REFUSED and the first in file order wins until it
# expires. (That guard is what lets a live controller take a run over from the
# script, which is deliberate.) moving.pad's header claimed the opposite for a
# while; it got the right answer anyway because its segments happen to be
# adjacent rather than overlapping. Every stick segment below is therefore
# strictly non-overlapping, and a diagonal is ONE event naming both directions
# (LUP+LRIGHT), never two overlaid. Buttons are unaffected -- digital ones OR
# and analog ones take the maximum -- so A and RT may overlap freely.
# test_pad_schedule.py asserts all of this.

# ── boot to the Corn tutorial (new_game.pad's groups, unchanged) ──
#
# Then bare A presses, which is the one thing not yet tried and the only change
# here that a measurement actually points at.
#
# Comparing a successful boot against a failed one, both reached the 1342-open
# plateau at t=36-37 -- so it is NOT that the failing run boots too slowly. The
# difference is what the A presses did afterwards. In the run that worked, the
# A at t=47 moved it 1342 -> 1344 -> 1408, i.e. that A selected New Game. In the
# run that failed, the A presses at t=39, 47, 55, 63 and 71 each did nothing at
# all.
#
# The suspicion that follows: once the menu is up, a group's START,START moves
# OFF it and the group's own A then lands somewhere harmless. So after the
# groups stop, press A alone -- no START -- a few times, spaced wide. If the
# menu is up, A is exactly what it wants. If the game is already in play, a
# bare A is a jump and costs nothing.
#
# This is a hypothesis with one comparison behind it, not a finding. If these
# bare presses do not change the hit rate, say so here and delete them.
#
# FIRST RUN WITH THEM: reached New Game (1408). That is 1 for 1 and means very
# little on its own -- the boot was already landing about two times in five.
# Note also that the host audio device failed in that run (CoreAudio -66681,
# which wedges repeatedly on this machine), so it does NOT test the live-device
# arm, whatever it looked like at launch. Someone needs to run this several
# times, with the AUDIO verdict read each time, before the bare presses can be
# called an improvement.
#
# Running tally across every run of the groups prefix, for whoever does that:
#   device dead/off    3 of 5 reached New Game
#   device live        0 of 2
# which is still too few to separate, and is why the audio note above is a
# retraction rather than a finding.
12.0:START  13.5:START  15.0:A
